Since the Taylor expansion is centered around the optical axis, it performs best around the center and worse in the extreme periphery. Unsurprisingly, polynomials of degree 3 or 5 consistently result in a smaller reconstruction error than the linear model (degree 1). Only for far off-axis rays, the benefits vanish, and the quality of higher-degree approximations drops below the one of the linear model. However, we note that such rays are outside the physically plausible range of the lens system and would be blocked by the barrel, which was neglected in our simulation.

