If compile times are a concern (for the anamorphic example with 861 flares, code generation and compilation takes about 8 minutes on a single CPU core), they can be further optimized. For instance, LLVM [ llv11 ] can be leveraged to directly produce a vectorized intermediate representation of the polynomial code (via llvm::IRBuilder), which can then be compiled and executed directly in memory (i.e. without parsing a source file). Also OpenCL compilers usually assume the kernels are small and can thus be optimized using very expensive techniques. These can be very finely controlled when manually compiling in memory.

